  <h2 id="outcomes">Where each admitted course actually leads</h2>

  <p>
    A seat is not the finish line. What the degree leads to over the next five to ten years matters more than the admission letter.
  </p>

  <p>
    The order below is not the conventional "closest to medicine" pecking order. Pharmacy leads because, among these course families, it combines the widest job market, the clearest ownership route, and the broadest ladder of paid specialisations — real scalability, not exam prestige. Agriculture follows for genuine, demand-led growth; biotechnology and general degrees sit last here only because their pay ceiling depends on a further specialisation or a deliberately built skill stack, not because they matter less.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {outcomeCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Owning a pharmacy is one of the clearest scalable paths in this whole list.</strong> A pharmacist who eventually runs their own outlet sets their own prices, can hire staff and open further locations, and earns from repeat, recurring customer visits — a ceiling most pure-employment tracks above do not offer by themselves.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pharmacy's competition does not end at admission, either. Getting a B.Pharm seat is the first competitive gate; reaching the higher-paying regulatory affairs, quality assurance, or clinical research roles usually needs an M.Pharm, and M.Pharm intake runs through its own competitive entrance exam (GPAT), with limited seats at top institutes. Plan for two separate competitive stages, not one.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    <strong>Build a high-income skill portfolio around the degree, not just the degree.</strong> A pharmacy, agriculture, biotech, or general-degree graduate who adds communication, data handling, regulatory or quality-systems literacy, and visible proof of work usually out-earns and out-lasts a peer who only holds the certificate.
  </p>

